27:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-what-is-a-context-switch",{"id":"01-what-is-a-context-switch","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"What a context switch actually is"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A context switch is the act of saving the CPU state of the thread that is currently running and restoring the CPU state of a different thread, so that one physical CPU can be shared among many threads over time. The word \"context\" means exactly the bundle of information that defines where a thread is in its execution: the contents of the registers, including the program counter that says which instruction is next. Freeze that context and you have frozen the thread; reload it later and the thread continues as if nothing ever interrupted it."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Here is why it must exist. The CPU has exactly one set of registers per core, but the OS is juggling far more threads than it has cores. Those registers are a shared resource that only one thread can occupy at a time. To run thread B while thread A is only half-finished, the OS has nowhere to keep A’s live registers except by copying them out to memory first, and it needs a place to put them. That place is the process (or thread) control block."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Context"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The complete CPU-visible state of a thread: its registers, program counter, stack pointer and status flags, everything needed to resume it exactly where it stopped."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Context switch"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Saving the current thread’s context and restoring another thread’s, so the CPU can change which thread it is executing."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"PCB (process control block)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The kernel’s per-process record; among other things it holds the saved register state (for threads, a per-thread control block does the same job)."}]]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Recall the timer interrupt from the hardware chapter. When it fires, the CPU is dragged out of user mode into the kernel, and the OS gets a chance to decide whether the current thread should keep running. If it decides to switch, this save-and-restore dance is what happens next. The timer is the heartbeat; the context switch is the actual changing of the guard."}],"$L29"]}],"$L2a"]}]]}],"$L2b","$L2c","$L2d","$L2e","$L2f"]}]
28:["$","$L30",null,{"prev":{"slug":"threads","title":"Threads","kind":"$undefined","number":9},"next":{"slug":"cpu-scheduling-basics","title":"CPU Scheduling I: The Basics","kind":"$undefined","number":11}}]
31:I[76156,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6238","static/chunks/app/operating-system/%5Bchapter%5D/page-6f5feff9906adfcd.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"NoteImage"]
29:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The one-sentence version",": "]}],"A context switch is \"dump every register into this thread’s control block, load the next thread’s registers back out of its control block, jump to its program counter\". Everything else in this chapter is detail on those two steps."]}]]}]
2a:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L31",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch10%2F01-what-is-a-context-switch.jpg?v=f973897d44abfb345b6adffff3217a0d","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: one CPU in the middle; thread A’s registers being dumped into its PCB box on the left while thread B’s saved registers are loaded from its PCB on the right; a timeline strip below showing A running, a thin switch sliver, then B running."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
2b:["$","section","02-what-gets-saved",{"id":"02-what-gets-saved","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Exactly what gets saved and restored"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The core of the saved context is the register file. Every general-purpose register (the ones holding your working values), the program counter, the stack pointer, and the status/flags register must be copied out to the thread’s control block. Miss even one and the thread resumes with corrupt state, a wrong value in a register or, worse, a program counter pointing at the wrong instruction. On real CPUs there is more to capture too: the floating-point and vector (SIMD) register state, which is large, so kernels often save it lazily and only when a thread actually uses it."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Now the crucial distinction. Switching between two threads of the same process is comparatively cheap, because those threads share one address space, the same page tables, the same view of memory. The OS swaps the registers and the stack pointer and it is essentially done. Switching between two different processes is more expensive, because on top of the registers the OS must also switch the memory map: it points the CPU at the new process’s page tables so that virtual addresses now translate to the new process’s physical frames."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Register state"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"General registers, program counter, stack pointer and flags, always saved and restored on any switch."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"FPU / SIMD state"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The large floating-point and vector register set; often saved lazily to avoid paying for it when a thread never touches it."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Address-space switch"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Repointing the CPU at a new process’s page tables. On x86 this means loading the CR3 register with the new page-table base."}]]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"On x86 that memory-map switch is concretely a write to the CR3 register, which holds the physical address of the current page table. Loading CR3 is the moment the machine stops seeing process A’s memory and starts seeing process B’s. As we will see in the cost section, that single register write has an expensive side effect on cached address translations, which is a big part of why a process switch hurts more than a thread switch."}],"$L32"]}],"$L33"]}]]}]
2c:["$","section","03-what-triggers-a-switch",{"id":"03-what-triggers-a-switch","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"What triggers a switch"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A context switch never happens for no reason. Something must pull the CPU into the kernel and give the scheduler an opening to change its mind about who should run. There are four everyday triggers, and it is worth being able to name each one when you see it in a trace."}],["$","ul","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Timer interrupt (preemption): the thread has used up its time slice, the timer fires, and the scheduler forcibly hands the CPU to someone else. This is what keeps one CPU-bound loop from freezing the machine."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Blocking system call / I/O wait: the thread asks for something that is not ready, a disk read, a network response, a lock held by another thread, so it cannot make progress. The kernel parks it and switches to a thread that can run."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"A higher-priority thread becomes ready: an interrupt or a wakeup makes a more important thread runnable. A preemptive scheduler will switch away from the current thread immediately rather than wait for its slice to end."}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Voluntary yield or exit: the thread explicitly gives up the CPU (a yield call, or waiting on a condition), or it simply finishes and terminates, and the OS must pick a successor."}]]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"It is useful to group these into two families. Involuntary (preemptive) switches, the timer tick and the higher-priority wakeup, are the OS taking the CPU away from a thread that would happily have kept running. Voluntary switches, blocking on I/O, yielding, exiting, are the thread giving the CPU up because it has nothing useful to do right now. The same save-and-restore machinery runs in every case; only the reason differs."}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Why the distinction matters",": "]}],"Involuntary switches usually mean \"too many CPU-hungry threads competing\", while a flood of voluntary switches usually means \"lots of blocking on I/O or locks\". When you diagnose a switch-heavy service, figuring out which family dominates points you straight at the fix."]}]]}]]}],"$L34"]}]]}]
2d:["$","section","04-dispatcher-and-latency",{"id":"04-dispatcher-and-latency","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The dispatcher & dispatch latency"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"It helps to split the work into policy and mechanism. The scheduler is the policy: it decides which thread should run next according to priorities, fairness, and deadlines. The dispatcher is the mechanism: it is the small, tightly-optimised piece of the kernel that actually carries out the switch once the scheduler has chosen. The scheduler answers \"who?\"; the dispatcher does the \"how\"."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Concretely, the dispatcher performs three jobs: it switches context (saving the outgoing thread’s registers and restoring the incoming thread’s), it switches to user mode if the new thread runs in user space, and it jumps to the correct location in the new thread’s code, that is, it reloads the program counter and lets execution resume there. It is deliberately kept lean, because it sits on the hot path of every single switch the system ever makes."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Scheduler"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The policy component that decides which thread should run next (covered in depth in the scheduling chapter)."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Dispatcher"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The mechanism that gives control of the CPU to the thread the scheduler chose: switch context, switch mode, jump to the thread’s next instruction."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Dispatch latency"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The time from when the scheduler decides to stop one thread to when the newly chosen thread actually begins executing."}]]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Dispatch latency is the gap between the decision and the resumption, pure overhead during which no useful work happens. For most software it is negligible, but for real-time and latency-sensitive systems it is a headline number: if an urgent thread becomes ready, the worst-case time before it actually runs depends directly on how small and how bounded the dispatch latency is. Keeping it low (and predictable) is a major goal of real-time kernel design."}],["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":["$L35","$L36"]}]]}],"$L37"]}]]}]
2e:["$","section","05-cost-of-switching",{"id":"05-cost-of-switching","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The cost of switching"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The direct cost of a context switch is the obvious part: the CPU cycles spent saving one register set, running a bit of scheduler logic, and restoring the next register set. On its own this is small, a fraction of a microsecond. If that were the whole story, switching would be cheap and we could switch as often as we liked. It is not the whole story."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The indirect cost is where the real damage lives, and it is all about the memory hierarchy from the hardware chapter. While thread A ran, it warmed the CPU caches with its own data and instructions. The instant thread B takes over, those caches are full of the wrong data; B runs slowly at first, suffering cache misses that stall the CPU on RAM until it re-warms the caches with its own working set. A process switch is worse still: loading CR3 to change the address space effectively invalidates the TLB (the cache of virtual-to-physical translations), so the new process pays a wave of expensive page-table walks before address translation gets fast again."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Direct cost"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The cycles to save and restore registers and run the scheduler/dispatcher, small and fixed per switch."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Indirect cost"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The performance lost afterwards to cold caches and (on a process switch) a flushed TLB, often far larger than the direct cost."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"TLB flush"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Discarding cached address translations when the address space changes; the new process must rebuild them via page-table walks."}]]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is why too-frequent switching quietly wrecks throughput, and why spawning more threads than you have cores can make a program slower rather than faster. Past the point where threads outnumber cores, adding threads does not add parallelism, it just adds context switches, and each switch throws away warm cache state. The CPU ends up spending its time re-warming caches instead of computing. This is exactly the situation profilers surface as high \"system\" or \"sys\" CPU time with disappointing real work done."}],"$L38","$L39","$L3a"]}],"$L3b"]}]]}]
2f:["$","section","06-mode-vs-context-switch",{"id":"06-mode-vs-context-switch","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Mode switch vs context switch, and takeaways"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Here is the confusion that trips up almost everyone. When your program makes a system call, the CPU flips from user mode into kernel mode, does the privileged work, and flips back. That is a mode switch, a change in privilege level, exactly the user/kernel boundary crossing we met in the hardware chapter. It is real work and it has a cost, but crucially it happens within the same thread. The same thread that called read() is the thread that returns from it, with its registers and address space intact."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A context switch is different: it changes which thread is running. A mode switch does not, by itself, cause a context switch. If read() finds its data already in the page cache, the kernel services the call and returns to the very same thread, one mode switch, zero context switches. A context switch only follows if that syscall has to block (the data is not ready), at which point the kernel parks your thread and dispatches another. So a blocking syscall can lead to a context switch, but the mode switch and the context switch are two separate events."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Mode switch"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A change of CPU privilege level (user ↔ kernel) within the same thread, what a system call, trap or interrupt causes."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Context switch"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A change of which thread the CPU is running, save one thread’s context, restore another’s."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Key relationship"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Every context switch runs through the kernel (so it involves kernel mode), but most mode switches are not context switches, they return to the same thread."}]]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Getting this right changes how you read performance data. A syscall-heavy workload pays mode-switch overhead on every call even when no thread ever changes; that is why batching I/O and avoiding a syscall per byte matters, as we noted with the mode boundary earlier. A switch-heavy workload pays the far larger context-switch tax of cold caches and TLB flushes. They are different problems with different fixes, and calling both \"context switching\" hides which one you actually have."}],"$L3c"]}],"$L3d"]}]]}]
32:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Remember this ratio",": "]}],"Thread switch within a process = swap registers only. Process switch = swap registers plus the whole address space. That asymmetry is exactly why threads are the cheaper unit of concurrency inside one program."]}]]}]
33:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L31",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch10%2F02-what-gets-saved.jpg?v=51cca45efe7d0101b091124706922cf0","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: left column lists the saved context, general registers R0..Rn, PC, SP, flags; a divider; right side shows a process switch also swapping the CR3 / page-table base register pointing to a different page table, with a note \"thread switch within same process = skip this part\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
34:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L31",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch10%2F03-what-triggers-a-switch.jpg?v=a632927931359ef4d300b040379d2038","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a running thread in the centre with four arrows pointing away, each labelled, timer interrupt (preemption), blocking syscall / I/O wait, higher-priority thread becomes ready, voluntary yield/exit; each arrow ends at \"scheduler picks next thread\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
35:["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}]
36:["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Split it in your head",": "]}],"Scheduler = who runs next (policy, hard decisions). Dispatcher = make it happen now (mechanism, fast and dumb). We give the scheduler its own chapter next; the dispatcher is the muscle that executes its verdict."]}]
37:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L31",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch10%2F04-dispatcher-and-latency.jpg?v=66b177eff78ab319b3a2d604cae5297c","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: two boxes side by side: \"Scheduler: WHO runs next? (policy)\" and \"Dispatcher: make it run NOW (mechanism)\"; below, a timeline bracket labelled \"dispatch latency\" spanning from the scheduling decision through save/restore/address-space switch to the first instruction of the new thread."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
38:["$","figure","4",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"vmstat shows system-wide context switches per second in the \"cs\" column"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ vmstat 1 3\n procs -----------memory---------- ---system-- ------cpu-----\n  r  b   swpd   free   buff  cache   in   cs  us sy id wa\n  3  0      0 1.2G   180M   3.1G  9200 48000  61 34  5  0\n  4  0      0 1.2G   180M   3.1G  9600 51200  59 37  4  0"}]}]]}]
39:["$","figure","5",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"pidstat -w breaks context switches down per process (cswch/s voluntary, nvcswch/s involuntary)"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ pidstat -w 1 1\n   UID     PID   cswch/s  nvcswch/s  Command\n  1000    4821   1120.00     880.00  app-server\n  1000    4990      3.00       2.00  logger"}]}]]}]
3a:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Engineer’s takeaway",": "]}],"A high cs rate with low useful work is a red flag. High cswch/s (voluntary) points to blocking on I/O or lock contention; high nvcswch/s (involuntary) points to CPU oversubscription. Size your thread pools near the core count for CPU-bound work, chasing more threads past that just pays switch tax."]}]]}]
3b:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L31",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch10%2F05-cost-of-switching.jpg?v=7f62514cb2feb9832cf67ed0684bcde4","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: two stacked bars, a thin \"direct cost: save + restore registers, run scheduler\" bar, and a much fatter \"indirect cost: cold caches refilling, TLB flush after CR3 load\" bar; a curve on the right showing throughput rising then falling as switch frequency grows, marked \"too many threads\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3c:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Recap & hand-off",": "]}],"A context switch saves one thread’s registers (and, across processes, the address space) and restores another’s; the dispatcher performs it after the scheduler chooses; its real cost is cold caches and TLB flushes, not the register copy; and a mode switch is a privilege flip, not a thread change. We now know how the CPU changes hands, next we tackle the harder question the scheduler answers: who should get it, and for how long."]}]]}]
3d:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L31",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch10%2F06-mode-vs-context-switch.jpg?v=eaf55d4925ecd2090052088f109d074b","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: left panel \"mode switch\", same thread crossing the user/kernel dashed line via a syscall and returning, register set unchanged; right panel \"context switch\", the dashed line plus the running thread being replaced by a different thread; a Venn-style note \"every context switch involves kernel mode, but not every mode switch is a context switch\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
